course: biology
lesson: 85
title: The ape house: orangutans, gibbons, bonobos and how apes think
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: An orangutan moves slowly across the ropes on arms longer than its legs, an arm span of over 2 metres, while a gibbon flies arm over arm so fast it looks like a swinging pendulum.
explain: Apes are clever in ways you can watch: an orangutan puts a sack on its head when it rains, apes tap puzzles on touch screens, and an orangutan that sees a mark on its face in a mirror reaches up to pick at it, so it knows the reflection is itself.
explain: Bonobos settle a quarrel with a hug instead of a fight, and in the morning the gibbons sing loud duets to say «this part of the forest is ours».
explain: Apes still cannot talk like us, because their throats and mouths are built differently, and King Louie singing in The Jungle Book is doubly made up: real orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra, not in India.
fact: Orangutans spend almost all their lives in trees; their arms are far longer than their legs and their arm span can be over 2 metres.
fact: Some grown-up male orangutans grow wide cheek pads called flanges, which show others they are strong grown-ups, and a throat sac that makes their «long call» carry through the forest.
fact: Wild orangutans hold big leaves over their heads like umbrellas, and zoo orangutans use sacks and blankets the same way.
fact: Wild orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra and are critically endangered, largely because forests were cut down for palm oil farms.
fact: Gibbons are small apes that sing loud duets, male and female together, heard about a kilometre away.
fact: Swinging arm over arm is called brachiation; gibbons are the fastest apes in the trees, reported at around 50 kilometres per hour.
fact: Bonobos are a separate kind of ape, slimmer than chimpanzees, and live only in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, south of the Congo River, while chimps live north of it; their groups are led by females and calm quarrels with hugs, grooming and sharing.
fact: Great apes do not swim naturally and can drown, so zoos use water moats around ape islands.
fact: Some zoos let apes play memory games on touch screens, and in a famous test young chimps remembered flashing numbers faster than university students.
fact: Apes cannot speak words; some, like the gorilla Koko and the bonobo Kanzi, learned hundreds of signs or symbols, but scientists argue about how much this is like language.
fact: In the mirror test, great apes, dolphins, elephants and magpies have noticed a mark on their own body, dogs and cats usually ignore it, and human babies usually pass at about 18 months.
fact: Like all apes, orangutans, gibbons and bonobos have no tails.
exercise: Why are an orangutan's arms so long? | To reach and hold branches high in the trees | To dig deep into the ground for roots and worms to eat | To swim across wide rivers with strong strokes | To carry heavy stones back to their tree nests
exercise: What are the big flat cheek pads of some male orangutans called? | Flanges | Gills | Pouches | Antlers
exercise: What does an orangutan's throat sac help with? | Making its long call carry through the forest | Storing fruit and nuts to eat later, like a hamster | Breathing underwater | Keeping its head warm
exercise: Why does an orangutan put a sack on its head in the rain? | It dislikes getting wet and uses things as umbrellas | It is trying to hide from the keeper so it can stay out all night | It is playing a hat game for visitors | The sack keeps flies away
exercise: Where do wild orangutans live? | Only on Borneo and Sumatra | All over Africa | In the jungles of India | In South America
exercise: What is the main threat to wild orangutans? | Forests cut down for palm oil farms | Too many visitors staring at them in zoos | Very cold, snowy winters in the forest | Being hunted and eaten by wild lions
exercise: Why do gibbons sing in the morning? | To tell other gibbons this part of the forest is theirs | Because they are hungry and want the keepers to bring their breakfast | To wake up the keepers | To scare away insects
exercise: What is brachiation? | Swinging arm over arm from branch to branch | Walking on the knuckles of the hands | Building a nest | Sleeping upside down
exercise: How do bonobos usually settle a quarrel? | With hugs, grooming and sharing food | With long, serious fights that leave them hurt | By leaving the group and never coming back | By beating their chests like gorillas
exercise: Who leads a bonobo group? | The females | The biggest male | The youngest bonobo | Nobody, they live alone
exercise: Why do zoos put water moats around ape islands? | Apes do not swim naturally and will not cross deep water | Apes like to sit on the bank all day and catch fish in the moat | Moats keep the apes cool in summer | The water stops visitors throwing food
exercise: Why do some zoos give apes touch screen games? | To study how apes think and to give them enrichment | To teach them how to use mobile phones and send messages | To keep them quiet for visitors | To sell the games to people
exercise: Why can't apes speak words? | Their throats and mouths are built differently | They are not clever enough to have any ideas at all | They speak, but too quietly for us | Keepers forbid them to talk
exercise: What did Koko the gorilla and Kanzi the bonobo learn? | Hundreds of hand signs or symbols | To speak whole sentences out loud in English | To read and write books of their own | To count all the way up to a million
exercise: What does the mirror test show? | Whether an animal knows the reflection is itself | Whether an animal can see colours | How fast an animal can run | Whether an animal likes the way it looks in the glass
exercise: At about what age do human babies usually pass the mirror test? | About 18 months | About 1 week | About 10 years | Never
exercise: Which of these animals have passed the mirror test? | Great apes, dolphins, elephants and magpies | Goldfish, ants, snails and garden worms | Only people | Cats, dogs and hamsters
test: In The Jungle Book, King Louie the orangutan sings in an Indian jungle. What would you fix? | Real orangutans live only on Borneo and Sumatra, and cannot speak | Nothing, orangutans are common in India and often sing to each other | Orangutans live in Africa, but they do sing | Orangutans live in South America
test: 2 orangutans, 3 gibbons and 4 bonobos. how many tails, think before u answer | 0 | 9 | 4 | 3
test: «bonobos are just chimps innit» What is right? | They are a separate kind, slimmer, led by females, south of the Congo | Yes, bonobo is just the baby name for a chimp before it grows up big and strong | Yes, chimps turn into bonobos when old | No, bonobos are a kind of monkey with a tail
test: How could a river make two kinds of ape grow apart? | Apes cannot swim, so groups on each side live apart for ages | The river water slowly changes the colour of the apes on one side of it | Apes on one side drink more water and grow bigger | Rivers have no effect on animals
test: «my ape got 10 out of 10 in a memory game, so it's smarter than me» Which is wiser? | Being good at one game does not mean being clever at everything | Yes, one game proves the ape is smarter than you in every single way there is | No, apes cannot remember anything | Memory games only measure eyesight
test: What would happen if you put a mark on a dog's face and showed it a mirror? | It usually ignores the mark, as dogs rarely pass this test | It always picks the mark off at once with its paw, like an ape would | It would draw a mark on the mirror | It would learn to speak
test: You are at a shop and see «palm oil from protected forests» on a label. Why could buying it help orangutans? | It supports farms that do not cut down their forests | Palm oil is fed to zoo orangutans | The money is posted straight to the apes in the forest | It makes palm trees grow faster in zoos
test: «can't the orangutan just swim off the island and escape» What is right? | Great apes do not swim naturally, so the moat keeps them in | Yes, orangutans are strong swimmers and cross rivers every day | Yes, but only at night | No, because the water is too hot
test: Which is the fastest way to cross a forest canopy for an ape? | A gibbon swinging arm over arm | An orangutan walking on the ground | A gorilla climbing slowly up the trunk | A bonobo hopping along on one leg
test: A cartoon gorilla says whole sentences to its keeper. What is closest to the truth? | Some apes learned many signs, but scientists argue if it is language | Gorillas really do speak words, but quietly and only to their own keepers | All apes can talk but choose not to | Apes understand no signs at all
test: A visitor counts 3 gibbon pairs singing duets. How many gibbons are singing? | 6 | 3 | 9 | 4
test: Why would a male orangutan want huge cheek pads? | They show others he is a strong, grown-up male | They store food for the long, hungry winter months | They help him float in rivers | They keep rain out of his ears
exam: Identify the principal threat to the survival of wild orangutans. | The clearance of their forests, largely for palm oil | Predation by lions upon the island of Borneo | The consumption of excessive quantities of fruit | The coldness of the winters on Sumatra
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of bonobo groups? | They are led by females and resolve disputes through hugging and grooming | They are led by a single dominant male who resolves disputes by combat | Each bonobo lives in solitude | They resolve disputes exclusively by vocal duets
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the capacity of apes for speech. | They cannot articulate words, though some have acquired many signs | They speak fluently, but only in the presence of their keepers | They possess grammar identical to that of humans | They are incapable of learning any sign whatsoever
exam: Determine what is demonstrated by an animal that, upon seeing a mark on its face in a mirror, touches that mark upon its own body. | That it apprehends the reflection to be itself | That it is incapable of perceiving colour | That it believes the reflection to be another animal | That it wishes to remove the mirror
